Pulmonary Embolism
Respiratory System
Sudden dyspnea, Pleuritic chest pain, Unexplained tachycardia, CTPA filling defect

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-039
Reason for Visit: Acute, sudden onset of unexplained shortness of breath at rest, sharp chest pain, and a racing heart rate requiring immediate life-saving evaluation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient who experienced a sudden, unprovoked onset of breathlessness and pleuritic chest pain while sitting down, accompanied by a rapid heart rate. This presentation is a time-critical medical emergency. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to immediately evaluate for a pulmonary embolism and facilitate rapid transport to the emergency department before cardiovascular collapse could occur.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I was just watching TV when suddenly I couldn't catch my breath, my heart started pounding, and now my chest hurts sharply when I breathe.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms began abruptly 2 hours ago. The patient reports a sudden, unexplained onset of dyspnea at rest without any preceding exertion. They describe sharp, pleuritic chest pain localized to the left lateral chest. The patient notes an unexplained fast heart rate (tachycardia) that has not resolved. They deny hemoptysis, fever, or productive cough. Upon direct questioning, the patient admits to mild, unilateral right calf swelling and tenderness over the past 3 days following a long car journey.
Functional Status: Previously fully independent; currently highly distressed and afraid to move.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of sudden cardiac arrest or syncope.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/65 mmHg | HR: 122 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 28 breaths/min | SpO2: 91% on room air | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Highly anxious, pale, taking rapid shallow breaths, visibly distressed.
Physical Exam: Chest auscultation reveals completely clear lung fields bilaterally with no wheeze, crackles, or rubs. The marked tachypnea and hypoxia (SpO2 91%) are entirely out of proportion to the normal lung auscultation. Cardiovascular exam demonstrates a regular but rapid tachycardia (122 bpm) with an elevated jugular venous pressure (JVP). Lower extremity examination reveals distinct unilateral swelling, warmth, and tenderness in the right calf, highly suspicious for a deep vein thrombosis (DVT).
Environmental Observations: Patient is clutching their chest; recently unpacked suitcase visible in the hallway.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications taken. No history of asthma or cardiac disease.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Pulmonary Embolism - Status: Exacerbated (Critical)
Differentials:

Spontaneous Pneumothorax - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral equal air entry with no hyperresonance on percussion, combined with the presence of unilateral calf swelling (DVT).

Acute Panic Attack - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Objective, unremitting hypoxia (SpO2 91%), unilateral calf swelling, and elevated JVP, which indicate true physiological cardiopulmonary compromise.

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain is strictly pleuritic rather than central/crushing, and the presence of DVT signs makes a pulmonary vascular event the primary suspect.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Critical

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with a textbook, highly lethal presentation of an acute Pulmonary Embolism: sudden unexplained dyspnea at rest, pleuritic chest pain, unexplained tachycardia, and objective hypoxia with completely clear lung fields. The presence of unilateral calf swelling (DVT) following a prolonged journey seals the clinical probability. The elevated JVP and borderline blood pressure indicate impending right ventricular failure and cardiovascular collapse. Deferring this assessment until the next in-hours period would almost certainly result in fatal cardiac arrest. An urgent after-hours home visit was required solely to recognize the critical vascular nature of the emergency, administer high-flow oxygen, and immediately activate a priority 1 ambulance transfer for CT pulmonary angiography (CTPA) and anticoagulation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Activated EMS (000) for immediate Priority 1 ambulance transport to the Emergency Department. Initiated continuous oxygen therapy at 4L/min via nasal prongs to improve oxygenation and reduce right heart strain. Kept the patient strictly immobilized.
Positive Behavioral Change: Remain completely still and lying semi-upright on the couch; minimizing your physical movement directly reduces the oxygen demand on your heart and physically prevents any further blood clots in your leg from breaking loose and traveling to your lungs.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable; immediate hospital admission required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover provided directly to the intensive care paramedics upon arrival.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Patient is being transferred via emergency ambulance immediately. The situation is critical and requires inpatient anticoagulant or thrombolytic therapy.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a life-threatening medical emergency; the diagnosis must be confirmed urgently at the hospital using a specialized contrast CT scan of your chest.
Clinical Course: You require immediate intravenous blood thinners in the emergency department to dissolve the clot and restore blood flow to your lungs.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not stand up, walk to the bathroom, or strain in any way while waiting for the ambulance.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A blood clot has formed in your swollen leg and a piece has broken off, traveling directly into your lungs and blocking the blood flow; this is severely straining your heart and preventing oxygen from getting into your body.
Actionable Steps: Focus entirely on taking slow, calm breaths while we wait for the paramedics; panicking increases your heart rate further, which puts additional dangerous stress on the right side of your heart.

